{#
    The entries of a `toctree` that prints itself in a document.

    Separate from `menu-item.html.twig` on purpose, and this is the whole of
    the difference: that one writes the rail, which is where a reader goes
    next, and this one writes a list standing in the prose. The core sends both
    through `menu-level.html.twig`, so a theme that overrides that one file has
    said the same thing about both — which is how a printed list came out as a
    row of filled rail items, every one marked as the current page.

    The link is built here rather than taken from the renderer. `renderLink`
    answers `#` for the document being rendered — its way of saying "you are
    here" — and a section of that document is that answer plus the anchor. Take
    the answer alone and an entry of the page it is on points at nothing.

    Recursion is the macro calling itself: a tree nests as deep as it has to,
    and the depth is the author's to choose with `:depth:`.
#}
{% macro entries(items, kids) %}
    {%- import _self as toc -%}
    <ul>
        {%- for item in items %}
            {%- set doc = renderLink(item.url) -%}
            {%- set href = (doc == '#' ? '' : doc) ~ (item.anchor ? '#' ~ item.anchor) -%}
            <li>
                <a href="{{ href ?: '#' }}">{{ item.value.toString }}</a>
                {%- set children = attribute(item, kids) -%}
                {%- if children %}{{ toc.entries(children, kids) }}{% endif -%}
            </li>
        {%- endfor %}
    </ul>
{% endmacro %}
